    "unit": "13 JAK Rifles, Indian Army",
    "year": "July 1999 (Kargil War (Operation Vijay) - Area of Dras, J&K)",
    "story": "The Hilltop Sentinel of Dras\n\nDuring the Kargil War, the capture of heavily fortified enemy positions on treacherous mountain peaks was crucial. Naik Subedar Sanjeev Kumar of 13 JAK Rifles exemplified unparalleled bravery during one such assault in the Dras sector. On a mission to clear a strategically vital, heavily defended enemy post, his platoon came under intense machine-gun and artillery fire. Undeterred, Naik Subedar Sanjeev Kumar led his section from the front, navigating through treacherous terrain and steep inclines. As they closed in, he faced a series of enemy bunkers that were spewing relentless fire, pinning down his men. Displaying extraordinary courage, he charged the first bunker single-handedly, lobbing grenades and engaging the enemy in close quarter combat, neutralizing it. Despite sustaining severe injuries in the process, he refused evacuation. Instead, he crawled towards the second bunker, which was still firing menacingly, and with sheer grit, he silenced it as well, eliminating its occupants. His heroic actions cleared the path for his platoon to advance and capture the objective. Naik Subedar Sanjeev Kumar's indomitable spirit, self-sacrifice, and raw courage under fire were instrumental in breaking the enemy's resolve and securing a critical victory for the Indian Army, inspiring his entire unit to push forward against overwhelming odds.\n\nQuote: \"\"Some objectives are worth fighting for, even if it means fighting alone.\"\"\n\nMoral: Even amidst the most brutal conditions, the will of a single soldier can overcome insurmountable obstacles. Leadership is not just about commanding, but about leading by example, even to the ultimate sacrifice."
